---
import LiveDot from './LiveDot.astro';
import SystemBandFigures from './SystemBandFigures.astro';
import changelog from '../../CHANGELOG.md?raw';
import { latestRelease } from '../lib/home/latest-release';
import { WINDOW_DAYS } from '../lib/ops/reads';
import { formatWindow } from '../lib/retention';

/**
 * The live system band on the home page (issue #437): the one place a visitor
 * who never opens /ops sees that this site is a running system in use.
 *
 * BUILT AT BUILD TIME EXCEPT FOR THE FIVE FIGURES. The frame, the lead cell,
 * the release tile and the fallback are prerendered with `/`; the figures are
 * src/components/SystemBandFigures.astro, deferred as a server island. The
 * release tile is the one tile that is a pure function of the commit, so it
 * has no reason to wait for a request, and building it here keeps it on the
 * page for a visitor without JavaScript.
 *
 * A BAR IN THE SHAPE OF THE CASE STUDY FACTS BAR SINCE THE COMPACT REVISION.
 * The first version was a heading row, a three-sentence paragraph and a grid of
 * 36px figures, about 470px tall at 1440px, and review found it outweighed the
 * lead story beneath it at every width. Each tile is now the facts bar's cell
 * (src/layouts/ArticleLayout.astro, 1j): the label in mono capitals above the
 * value, `py-[22px]`, full-bleed bottom rule, and the heading moved into a lead
 * cell of the same grammar rather than sitting above the grid.
 *
 * SEVEN CELLS, AND EVERY BREAKPOINT FILLS ITS TRACKS EXACTLY: an empty track in
 * a gap-over-rule grid paints as a block of rule colour rather than as
 * nothing. The lead cell spans the whole first row below `lg` (two figure
 * columns on a phone, three from `md`), spans two rows beside a 3×2 block at
 * `lg`, and takes one wider track of a single row from `xl`. The single row
 * waits for `xl` because at 1024px seven tracks leave a figure cell about 80px
 * of text, and the longest labels break onto four lines there.
 */
const release = latestRelease(changelog);

/**
 * The fallback's labels, in the island's order. They have to match the
 * labels SystemBandFigures renders, or the swap moves a label from one cell to
 * another; tests/system-band.test.ts reads both.
 */
const LABELS = [
  'Public MCP tool calls',
  'Chat sessions',
  'Requests from agents',
  'AI Gateway requests',
  'Edge compute requests',
];

/**
 * A tile, the label and the value line. SystemBandFigures repeats all three
 * for the island's tiles, so the fallback and the figure occupy one box.
 *
 * `px-5` below `lg` is the page gutter, so a figure in the left column lines up
 * with the text above and below the band; from `lg` the lead cell holds the
 * left column and the tiles take the facts bar's `px-6`. `mt-auto` sits the
 * value on the tile's floor, so values line up across a row when one label
 * wraps and its neighbour does not.
 *
 * `leading-6` IS ON EVERY ARM OF THE VALUE LINE: the figure, the absence and
 * the fallback's empty line. The absence is set smaller than the figure, and
 * without a shared line height the swap would move a row by the difference.
 * `auto-rows-fr` on the grid did that job before, when the absence borrowed
 * `OpsMetric`'s `text-heading` against a `text-figure` value: measured in
 * Chrome on 2026-09-26, a row of absences was 7px shorter than a row of
 * fallbacks. With one line height there is no difference left to equalise.
 */
const CELL = 'flex flex-col gap-2 bg-bg px-5 py-[22px] lg:px-6';
const LABEL = 'font-mono text-micro uppercase text-ink-muted';
const VALUE = 'mt-auto font-display text-subheading leading-6 text-ink';
const ABSENT = 'mt-auto font-mono text-small leading-6 text-ink-muted';
---

<section data-system-band aria-labelledby="system-band-heading" class="border-b border-rule">
  <div class="group/band mx-auto grid max-w-[1440px] grid-cols-2 gap-px bg-rule md:grid-cols-3 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1.4fr)_repeat(3,minmax(0,1fr))] xl:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1.5fr)_repeat(6,minmax(0,1fr))]">
    {/*
        The lead cell: the band's name, the window its figures cover, and the
        way out to /ops. Its label line and its value line are the tiles' own
        type, so it reads as the first cell of the bar rather than a caption
        over it. `lg:pl-10` is the page gutter at that width, which the tiles'
        `px-6` is not. `data-system-band-links` is a container rather than the
        link itself because the how-it-works issue adds a second link to it.
      */}
    <div
      data-band-lead
      class="col-span-2 flex flex-col gap-2 bg-bg px-5 py-[22px] md:col-span-3 lg:col-span-1 lg:row-span-2 lg:pl-10 lg:pr-6 xl:row-span-1"
    >
      {/*
          THE DOT IS GREEN ONLY WHILE A LIVE FIGURE IS ON THE PAGE, the rule
          /ops's status square keeps: that square turns amber when the page
          degrades rather than always reading nominal. This frame is
          prerendered and cannot know whether the island arrived, so the
          selector asks the page instead. `group-has-[…]/band` matches once a
          `data-band-figure` tile holds a `data-numeric` value, which only the
          island renders and only for a figure it read. Before the island
          arrives, without JavaScript, or with every read absent, the dot is
          muted ink and still. The release tile is `data-numeric` too, which
          is why the selector names `data-band-figure` and not the grid.

          The dot itself, its colours and its pulse, is
          src/components/LiveDot.astro, shared with /ops's masthead.
        */}
      <p data-band-live class:list={['flex items-center gap-2', LABEL]}>
        <LiveDot state="until-live" />
        <span>Live · last {formatWindow(WINDOW_DAYS)}</span>
      </p>
      <h2 id="system-band-heading" class="font-display text-subheading text-ink">
        How humans+agents use this site
      </h2>
      <div data-system-band-links class="mt-auto flex flex-wrap gap-5 pt-1">
        <a href="/ops#live-metrics" class="font-mono text-micro uppercase text-accent">
          All metrics &rarr;
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>

    <SystemBandFigures server:defer>
      <Fragment slot="fallback">
        {/*
            NO FIGURE IN THE FALLBACK, AND THAT IS WHAT MAKES IT HONEST.
            This is what a visitor without JavaScript, a crawler and the AI
            Search index read, and a count baked in at build time would be a
            stale number presented as a live one. Each cell keeps its label
            and holds the figure's space empty: `h-[1lh]` on an element set
            in the figure's own type is exactly one line of that type, so
            the tile arriving does not move the label.
          */}
        {LABELS.map((label) => (
          <div data-band-fallback={label} class={CELL}>
            <p class={LABEL}>{label}</p>
            <p aria-hidden="true" class:list={['h-[1lh]', VALUE]} />
          </div>
        ))}
      </Fragment>
    </SystemBandFigures>

    {/*
        The release tile, in the band's two lines: what it is, and the
        figure. The date rides on the label line rather than a third line of
        its own, which would lift this value above its neighbours'. `whitespace-nowrap`
        on the date because the label wraps in a narrow track, and at 768px and
        1024px it broke the date at its own hyphens. Its source
        is the link. A DATE AND NEVER A TIME, which `recentReleases` already
        enforces (10 §1.4).

        THE ABSENCE HAS NO LINK. `latestRelease` returns `null` when the
        changelog has no `## ` heading or its first one does not parse,
        rather than falling through to an older release. A link built from
        a version that is not there would point at a release that does not
        exist, so the null arm is the same muted absence the figures render,
        in the same box.
      */}
    <div data-release-tile class="grid bg-bg">
      {release === null ? (
        <div class={CELL}>
          <p class={LABEL}>Latest site release</p>
          <p class={ABSENT}>not available</p>
        </div>
      ) : (
        <a href={release.url} class:list={[CELL, 'no-underline']}>
          <p class={LABEL}>
            Latest site release:{' '}
            <time datetime={release.date} class="whitespace-nowrap">
              {release.date}
            </time>
          </p>
          <p class={VALUE} data-numeric>
            {release.version}
          </p>
        </a>
      )}
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
